A budget analysis based on Norway, Sweden, Denmark and major republics
Central thesis
From a monarchy-friendly perspective, the core argument is as follows: modern constitutional monarchies, particularly within the Scandinavian model, maintain the symbolic head-of-state office at a relatively low and predictable cost. In republics, by contrast, the head-of-state office often comes with a larger institutional structure, heavier political security burden, higher transition costs, and a more extensive bureaucratic apparatus. The claim that "monarchy is expensive and republic is cheap" is therefore not universally valid – on the contrary, the monarchy can in certain countries offer a more stable and more cost-effective model. This applies especially to narrow, constitutional and ceremonial monarchies such as Norway, Sweden and Denmark.
1) Current court/royal family budgets of the Scandinavian monarchies
Norway (2026, approved budget):
In Norway, the approved budget for 2026 shows a total amount of NOK 307,710,000 for "The Royal Household". This includes the appanage for the King and Queen, the operating expenses of the Royal House/court institution, and a separate appanage for the Crown Prince couple. In the official table, the appanage for the King and Queen is stated as NOK 16,234,000, the court institution''s share as NOK 277,963,000, and the appanage for the Crown Prince couple as NOK 13,513,000.
Sweden (2026):
In the Swedish government''s budget document for 2026, the proposed amount for "Kungliga hov- och slottsstaten" is SEK 190,457,000. This item covers both the official tasks of the Royal House and the Royal Castles administration as a central budget chapter.
Denmark (Finance Bill proposal 2026):
In the Danish Ministry of Finance''s proposal for the Finance Bill for 2026, DKK 134.6 million is allocated to the King and DKK 19.4 million to "other members of the royal family". The total visible expenditure framework for the Royal House amounts to DKK 154.0 million. As this is based on a proposal document, the figures should be read as official proposals and not as a final adopted budget.
Read in context, these three examples show that the cost of the head-of-state office in the Scandinavian monarchies is not "uncontrolled palace costs", but fixed, transparent and predictable public expenditure. Particularly in Norway and Sweden, the items are clearly visible in the budget.
2) Major republics as comparison basis
The examples below are chosen from the main republics with open budgets, to enable a comparison of the core structure of the palace/state leadership. The scope varies from country to country; some include only the operation of the official residence, others a broader presidential office. The figures are therefore not directly comparable one-to-one, but still provide a directional indication.
USA (budget request 2026):
The budget request for the "Executive Residence" at the White House for 2026 is USD 16.1 million. The congressional report shows that the appropriation for 2025 was USD 15.453 million, the request for 2026 is USD 16.1 million, and the committee''s recommendation is USD 16.0 million. This item applies exclusively to operating expenses for the President''s residence – the Secret Service, Air Force One, and the President''s other security/operational structure are not included.
France (2025):
According to the performance document submitted to the National Assembly, the requested amount for the Élysée / Présidence de la République for 2025 is EUR 125,662,386. The same appendix to the National Assembly states that the appropriation for 2025 is estimated to remain stable at around EUR 123 million; however, the detailed operating amount in the performance document is EUR 125.66 million.
Germany (2026):
In the German federal budget for 2026, the total for "Bundespräsident und Bundespräsidialamt" is stated as EUR 67.388 million. This is the total expenditure framework for the Federal President and the Presidential Office.
Italy (2025):
In the Quirinale''s budget note for 2025, the annual state appropriation for the Presidency is stated as EUR 230 million. The document also indicates a separate supplementary financing framework for infrastructure and technical works for the period 2025–2027.
Finland (2026):
In the Finnish budget proposal for 2026, the operating expenses for the Office of the President of the Republic are stated as EUR 9,775,000, the appropriation for official visits and travel as EUR 1,950,000, and the appropriation for expenses for former presidents as EUR 550,000. These open presidential items alone thus amount to a double-digit million sum in euros.
Iceland (2026):
In Iceland, the total appropriation for 2026 for "Embætti forseta Íslands" is ISK 418.7 million. This example shows that the head-of-state office is not "free" either in a Scandinavian republic with a small population.
Turkey (2026):
In the Turkish Strategy and Budget Directorate''s financial overview for central administration budgeted institutions for 2026, the visible items for "Cumhurbaşkanlığı" (Presidency Secretariat) total TRY 16,495,534,000. In the Stortinget committee meeting minutes, however, a broader framework of TRY 21,286,534,000 is also referred to in connection with discussions on the presidential budget; it is therefore necessary to distinguish between the narrow institutional budget line and the broader institutional framework. The most conservative and direct reading of the official table basis is the institution''s line item of TRY 16.495 billion.
3) Monarchy-friendly interpretation: Why the Scandinavian model appears robust
The core budget-friendly argument for the monarchy is as follows: the Scandinavian monarchies are head-of-state institutions without presidential elections and presidential transitions, with a low level of political tension, functioning ceremonially and continuously. This structure limits expenditure related to security needs during election periods, costs of office transition, building new staffs, politicised protection burdens, and institutional competition surrounding the presidential office. The official figures for Norway, Sweden and Denmark confirm that these institutions operate according to a logic of "stable state costs".
Looking at the republic examples, however, the budgets are significant even in countries with a symbolic head-of-state office – Germany, France and Italy are clear examples of this. The claim that "abolishing the monarchy will yield large savings" thus cannot be automatically verified with figures, at least not for European state types.
4) What would the cost be if Norway were a republic?
No secure official figures exist here – since Norway is not a republic. The following is therefore of a comparative and analytical nature. The estimate is based on Norway''s current monarchy budget and the open head-of-state chapters in countries such as Finland, Iceland and Germany.
Norway''s current monarchy costs:
Norway''s total royal budget for 2026 is NOK 307,710,000.
Which expenses would persist in a republic scenario?
Even without the Royal House, Norway would still need a head of state, an official residence, official receptions and ceremonies, foreign visits, a personnel staff, an archive and protocol structure, security and maintenance expenses. In Finland, the items for the office, travel and former presidents clearly amount to hundreds of millions of euros; in Germany, the federal presidency including the associated offices is EUR 67.388 million.
Reasonable estimate:
If Norway were a republic, it is highly likely that the annual cost – even in a ceremonial presidency model – would be very close to the current level, and possibly somewhat higher due to security, institution-building and transition costs. A cautious monarchy-friendly estimate suggests that Norway''s stabilised annual cost in a republic scenario could fall in the range of NOK 320–450 million, and that during the transition period one-off expenses will additionally be incurred for new institutional design, symbolic changes, protocol restructuring and security reorganisation. This is not an official figure, but an analytical estimate derived from the comparisons with Finland, Iceland and Germany.
The monarchy-friendly conclusion is clear: The primary gain from introducing a republic in Norway would not be a large saving in the budget, but exclusively a constitutional and principled regime change. On the cost side, a significant reduction is not guaranteed – on the contrary, the republic in low-politicised and ceremonial monarchies such as Norway''s may in fact end up costing more.
5) Conclusion
In light of the Scandinavian constitutional monarchies, the royal houses are not "waste machines" – they are in most cases fixed public institutions with known expenses, free from political transition stress, and with a diplomatic representation function. The data from Norway, Sweden and Denmark support this.
The head-of-state office in republics is not always cheaper either. Just the "Executive Residence" at the White House in the USA is an item of many millions of dollars; the examples from France, Germany, Italy, Finland, Iceland and Turkey show that the head-of-state institution in republics has a budget that should not be underestimated. The opponents'' claim that "abolishing the monarchy will yield large savings" is – particularly for Norway''s part – not convincingly supported by budget data.
The strongest monarchy-friendly argument specifically for Norway is: the existing system already operates at a relatively limited cost; a transition to republic would be an ideological change, not an economic revolution.', null, '[]'), (279, 'Title: FactShield OSINT Report: Rebuttal of Disinformation against H.K.H. Princess Sofia of Sweden through Court Documents', '**ANALYST:** NorthByte Analyst Dogukan Yegin', 'Introduction:
Recently, various media channels and internet platforms have used leaked Jeffrey Epstein documents (DOJ / EFTA) to produce baseless claims against H.K.H. Princess Sofia of the Swedish royal family. Open-Source Intelligence (OSINT) data extraction conducted by FactShield mathematically and documentarily proves that Princess Sofia was not part of this dark network; her name was exclusively reported to Epstein by third parties.

Findings (Innocence Analysis):

1. Absence of direct communication and the "third-party factor":
In none of the leaked documents is there a direct email sent by Princess Sofia to Jeffrey Epstein. All correspondence mentioning her name consists of "social updates" given to Epstein by third parties, such as Barbro Ehnbom and her son Marcus Forss. For example, in an email written by Marcus Forss on June 18, 2010, the phrase "Sofia Hellqvist (who I believe you have met)..." is used, which confirms, within their own network, that the acquaintance between Sofia and Epstein was very superficial.

2. Context and exploitation of the "Africa picture" (February 7, 2010):
The photograph manipulated by the media, showing Princess Sofia at a computer in Africa (EFTA00775965.pdf), was not sent by Sofia herself, but by Barbro Ehnbom to Epstein. In the relevant email, Ehnbom wrote: "Here is a photo of our Sofia - you remember - i.e., soon Princess Sofia... the entire Swedish press is looking for her... while she is in Africa!". This document does not prove that Sofia sent private photos to Epstein, but that Barbro Ehnbom attempted to exploit popular figures in her own circle (name-dropping) to impress Epstein.

3. The biggest media scandal: The geographical mistake "Sofia, Bulgaria":
The document "EFTA02011440.pdf", which trolls and media mistook for a secret connection to the Swedish Princess Sofia, has been completely refuted through OSINT analysis. The subject line of this email dated October 22, 2012, is clearly "Hello from Sofia Bulgaria". The word "Princess" mentioned in the message does not refer to the Swedish Princess Sofia, but to another person/princess located in Bulgaria''s capital, Sofia. Confusing the name of a geographical capital with the name of a Swedish individual proves how ignorant and malicious the current smear campaign is.

Conclusion:
Official court records confirm that the Swedish Princess Sofia was not in an organized communication network with Jeffrey Epstein. She was a passive "social figure" that third parties (especially Barbro Ehnbom) used to prove their own narcissistic power networks to Epstein. These claims are a disinformation operation lacking data literacy.

