Analyst & Founder, Nordic Fact Shield Norway', null, '[]'), (292, 'General Rule

OSINT (Open Source Intelligence) is legal in Norway, as long as the information collected is publicly available and no form of unauthorized access to systems is undertaken.', 'Analyst: Analyst: Analyst Doğukan Yegin Nordic Fact Shield Norway (Powered by Northbyte Norway Tec Ltd.)', 'General Rule

OSINT (Open Source Intelligence) is legal in Norway, as long as the information collected is publicly available and no form of unauthorized access to systems is undertaken.

✅ Permitted Use (Legal OSINT)

The following activities are considered legal:

Collection of information from open sources (websites, search engines)
Analysis of publicly available data
Use of social media where the content is public
Lookups in open registers (WHOIS, public databases)
Analysis of publicly available codebases (e.g., GitHub)
❌ Prohibited Actions

The following activities are not permitted and may be punishable:

Unauthorized access to computer systems
Attempts to bypass authentication (login bypass)
Brute-force attacks or password attempts
Active vulnerability scanning without permission
Exploitation of security holes (exploits) without consent
Access to non-public information
⚖️ Relevant Legislation

OSINT must be assessed against the following laws in Norway:

The Penal Code
§ 204: Unauthorized access to computer systems
§ 205: Data breach
The Personal Data Act
Regulates the processing of personal data (GDPR)
The Security Act
Protects national security interests and critical infrastructure
⚠️ Privacy (GDPR)

Even if information is publicly available, the following applies:

Personal data must be handled with caution
Mass collection and profiling may be illegal
Further distribution of sensitive data may be punishable
🧠 Important Principle

If access requires authentication, permission, or technical circumvention, it is no longer considered OSINT.

🔐 Summary
OSINT is legal in Norway
Only passive collection of open data is permitted
Any form of active access or testing without permission is illegal', null, '[]'), (293, 'Personal and Ethical Reflection on Autism and Society', 'Analyst: Analyst: Analyst: analyst Doğukan Yegin Nordic Fact Shield Norway (Powered by Northbyte Norway Tec Ltd.)', 'Personal and Ethical Reflection on Autism and Society

I am a legally recognized autistic individual. Throughout my life, I have never been part of a "normal" social circle. I have lived as an outsider—one who stands apart from the majority.

For over three decades, I have experienced bullying, stigmatization, and being called "crazy", "sick", or "disabled" simply because my brain functions differently. Yet I have never harmed anyone or committed a crime.

Instead, I chose a different path.

I built my own life. I founded a technology company in England (Northbyte Norway TEC Ltd.) and today work with digital security and OSINT to contribute to the protection of Scandinavian societies and institutions.

I am not a threat. I am an autistic person who has learned to live with his own reality.

I have consciously chosen to distance myself from environments that did not accept me, and I focus fully on my work, my discipline, and my future. What I want is simple: peace, stability, and a dignified life without prejudice.

This is not a complaint. It is an honest account.

Ethical Stance and Analytical Method

I am committed to ethical principles. I explicitly distance myself from disinformation, fake news, and conspiracy theories. I do not believe anything blindly.

Whether a person is guilty or innocent is not determined by tabloids or unconfirmed media reports, but by:

open sources
official registers
publicly available documents
verifiable communication

In my work, therefore:

verifiability
transparency
evidence-based analysis

are fundamental principles.

Case Analysis: Arion Kurtaj

The case of Arion Kurtaj is often misinterpreted in the public eye.

It is documented that the individual in question performed unauthorized access against companies including:

Rockstar Games
Uber
Nvidia

Sensitive data was obtained and used in attempts at leaks and extortion.

It has also been established that security systems were breached repeatedly through illegal access.

